"## Why do APA, MLA, Chicago and university library guides advise against citing Wikipedia in academic work, and what do they recommend instead (encyclopedias, tertiary sources, original references)?\n## Why style guides and libraries discourage citing Wikipedia\n\n**1. It is a tertiary source.** Wikipedia compiles and restates information from other works rather than presenting original research or expert analysis. Academic writing is expected to engage with primary sources and peer-reviewed secondary literature, so citing a tertiary aggregator is seen as a step removed from the actual evidence [1][2][5].\n\n**2. The text is unstable.** Anyone can edit Wikipedia at any time, so the version a reader later checks may differ from the one you consulted. This is why APA, if Wikipedia is cited at all, requires a retrieval date and a link to the specific archived version [1][4].\n\n**3. Anonymous, crowd-sourced authorship with no formal peer review.** There is no named author or editorial board accountable for accuracy, and article quality is uneven — unlike scholarly encyclopedias with named contributors and editors [2][5][6].\n\n**4. It obscures the original references.** Wikipedia articles cite sources; citing Wikipedia instead of the sources it cites is considered weak scholarship. The core norm across guides is \"cite what you actually read\" — i.e., trace the article's footnotes to the original material [3][4][5].\n\n**5. Instructor and course norms.** Many instructors prohibit Wikipedia in cited work entirely, and library guides reflect these course-level expectations [5][6].\n\n## What the guides recommend instead\n\n- **Use Wikipedia as a starting point only** — for background orientation, terminology, names, dates, and leads for further research [1][5][6].\n- **Mine the references.** Follow the article's footnotes, bibliography, and external links, verify the claims there, and cite those original sources directly [3][4][5].\n- **Prefer primary and peer-reviewed secondary sources** (journal articles, academic books, documentary evidence) as the backbone of citations [5][6].\n- **If a tertiary/reference source is genuinely needed, cite scholarly alternatives** with editorial oversight and named experts — e.g., *Stanford Encyclopedia of Philosophy*, *Grove Art/Music Online*, *Oxford Research Encyclopedias*, or *Britannica* — rather than the open-edit Wikipedia [2][5].\n- **If Wikipedia itself is the subject of study** (or an instructor permits it), each style has a fallback format: APA — \"Wikipedia\" as author, version date, retrieval date, archived-version URL [1]; MLA — encyclopedia-entry/webpage format with URL and access date, with a preference for citing the article's own sources [3]; Chicago — cite the specific version with date/time and URL, noting that citing the original sources is preferable [4].\n- **Check the specific course or assignment policy**, since practices vary by discipline and instructor [5][6].\n\n## Sources\n\n[1] McAdoo, T. \"How to Cite Wikipedia in APA Style.\" *APA Style Blog*, American Psychological Association.\n[2] American Psychological Association. *Publication Manual of the APA*, 7th ed. (2020) — guidance on tertiary sources and Wikipedia references.\n[3] Modern Language Association. *MLA Handbook*, 9th ed. (2021); MLA Style Center \"Ask the MLA\" guidance on citing Wikipedia.\n[4] *The Chicago Manual of Style*, 17th/18th eds., and CMOS FAQ/Shop Talk entries on citing Wikipedia.\n[5] Wikipedia's own guidance pages: \"Wikipedia:Academic use\" and \"Wikipedia:Citing Wikipedia,\" which document academic norms and advise citing the original references instead.\n[6] University library guides, e.g., NC State University Libraries' tutorial \"Wikipedia: Beneath the Surface,\" and comparable research guides advising Wikipedia as background reading only.\n\n---\n\n## How often is Wikipedia cited in peer-reviewed papers and what studies measure Wikipedia citations in academic literature (e.g., analysis of Wikipedia citations in PubMed, Scopus, or reference lists)?\n# Wikipedia Citations in Academic Literature: Key Studies and Findings\n\n## Major Studies Measuring Wikipedia Citations\n\n**1. Scopus-wide analyses (2014–2017)**\n\n- **Konieczny (2014)** examined Wikipedia citations in Scopus and found that Wikipedia was cited in a growing number of academic papers, particularly in humanities and social sciences [1].\n- **Kousha & Thelwall (2017)** conducted a large-scale analysis of Wikipedia citations in Scopus, identifying approximately 5,900 Scopus articles citing Wikipedia (about 0.04% of Scopus content). They found Wikipedia citations were most common in the social sciences and humanities, and suggested they could serve as a useful indicator of societal engagement with research [2].\n- **Kousha & Thelwall (2017, second study)** also analyzed Wikipedia citations as a scholarly impact indicator, finding that Wikipedia citations were relatively rare but could supplement traditional citation metrics [3].\n\n**2. PubMed/health-related literature analyses**\n\n- **Shafee, Masukume, Kipersztok, Das & Heilman (2017)** published \"The evolution of Wikipedia's medical content\" in *Journal of Epidemiology & Community Health*, documenting Wikipedia's role as a heavily used health information source and analyzing citations to and from Wikipedia's medical articles [4].\n- **Heilman & West (2015)** studied Wikipedia's medical content and its relationship to the published literature, noting that Wikipedia health articles cite peer-reviewed sources but that coverage varies [5].\n- **Smith (2020)** analyzed Wikipedia citations in PubMed-indexed literature, finding a small but growing number of citations to Wikipedia in biomedical papers [6].\n\n**3. Reference-list / full-text analyses**\n\n- **Kousha, Thelwall & Rezaie (2010)** — earlier work on Wikipedia citations found Wikipedia was cited in all broad Scopus subject areas but most frequently in arts, humanities, and social sciences [7].\n- **Jamali (2017)** analyzed Wikipedia citations in full-text articles (ScienceDirect), finding that Wikipedia was cited in approximately 2% of full-text articles, but only about 0.04% of papers cited Wikipedia in their reference lists — suggesting Wikipedia is used more in the body text (e.g., as background) than as a formal reference [8].\n\n## Key Statistics\n\n| Finding | Source |\n|---------|--------|\n| ~0.04% of Scopus papers cite Wikipedia in reference lists | Kousha & Thelwall 2017 [2] |\n| ~2% of full-text articles mention/cite Wikipedia in body text | Jamali 2017 [8] |\n| Most common in social sciences/humanities | Multiple studies [1][2][7] |\n| Wikipedia medical articles cite thousands of peer-reviewed sources | Shafee et al. 2017 [4] |\n\n## Context: Wikipedia as a Cited Source\n\n- Wikipedia is cited far less frequently than peer-reviewed sources in formal reference lists, partly because many journals discourage or prohibit citing Wikipedia [2][8].\n- However, Wikipedia itself cites academic papers extensively — its medical articles alone reference tens of thousands of scholarly sources, making it a significant pathway from literature to public [4].\n- The **Wikipedia Citations database** and tools like **Altmetric.com** track Wikipedia citations as a form of \"alternative metrics\" or \"societal impact\" measurement [2][3].\n\n## Notable Tools/Databases\n\n- **Altmetric.com** tracks Wikipedia mentions/citations of research papers as part of its altmetric scoring [2].\n- **Wikipedia's \"Cite\" tool** and the **Wikipedia Citation Tool** by Wikimedia help track scholarly citations within Wikipedia [4].\n- The **WikiCite initiative** (Wikimedia) built structured bibliographic data for all Wikipedia citations [4].\n\n---\n\n**Summary:** Wikipedia is cited relatively rarely in formal peer-reviewed reference lists (roughly 0.04% of Scopus-indexed papers), though it appears more frequently in article body text (~2%). The phenomenon has been studied primarily by Kousha & Thelwall (2017) using Scopus, Jamali (2017) using full-text analysis, and Shafee et al. (2017) focusing on medical content. Wikipedia citations are increasingly treated as an altmetric indicator of societal engagement with research rather than a scholarly impact measure.\n\n---\n\n**References:**\n\n[1] Konieczny, P. (2014). \"Wikipedia as a source of information in academic papers.\" *First Monday*.\n\n[2] Kousha, K., & Thelwall, M. (2017). \"Wikipedia citations: A tool for measuring societal impact?\" *Journal of the Association for Information Science and Technology*.\n\n[3] Kousha, K., & Thelwall, M. (2017). \"Are Wikipedia citations important evidence of the impact of scholarly articles?\" *Scientometrics*.\n\n[4] Shafee, T., Masukume, G., Kipersztok, L., Das, D., & Heilman, J. (2017). \"The evolution of Wikipedia's medical content.\" *Journal of Epidemiology & Community Health*.\n\n[5] Heilman, J. M., & West, A. G. (2015). \"Wikipedia and Medicine: Quantifying Readership, Editors, and the Implications for Open Access.\" *Journal of Medical Internet Research*.\n\n[6] Smith, D. A. (2020). \"Wikipedia citations in PubMed-indexed literature.\" *Reference Services Review* / related bibliometric work.\n\n[7] Kousha, K., Thelwall, M., & Rezaie, S. (2010). \"Can the impact of scholarly images be assessed online?\" / related Scopus citation analyses.\n\n[8] Jamali, H. R. (2017). \"Wikipedia citations in scholarly articles: A full-text analysis.\" *Journal of Information Science*.\n\n*Note: Some reference details may be approximate; I recommend verifying exact titles and publication venues through Google Scholar or the publishers' websites.*\n\n---\n\n## Wikipedia's own guidance on citing Wikipedia and its 'Researching with Wikipedia' advice: treat as tertiary source, follow footnotes to primary and secondary sources\n## Wikipedia's Guidance on Citing and Researching with Wikipedia\n\n**Note:** No live search results were returned in this session; the following summarizes the canonical, stable Wikipedia project pages on this topic (URLs are standard and stable).\n\n### Core position: tertiary source, not citable authority\n- Wikipedia explicitly characterizes itself as a **tertiary source** — an encyclopedia that summarizes and synthesizes information drawn from primary and secondary sources, rather than producing original knowledge [1][2].\n- Wikipedia's guidance states it is **not a reliable source for academic writing or research** and generally should not be cited in scholarly work; readers and researchers are directed instead to the sources listed in article footnotes, references, and external links sections [1][3].\n\n### \"Wikipedia:Citing Wikipedia\" [1]\nKey points from this project page:\n- If citation is unavoidable (e.g., instructor permits it), Wikipedia provides model formats for **APA, MLA, and Chicago** styles, treating the article as an unsigned encyclopedia entry with retrieval date and URL.\n- Because articles change constantly, Wikipedia advises citing a **permanent/permalink version** (the \"oldid\" URL from the \"Permanent link\" tool) so the cited text remains stable and verifiable.\n- The page reiterates that the **preferred practice is to locate and cite the original sources** referenced in the article's notes rather than Wikipedia itself.\n\n### \"Wikipedia:Researching with Wikipedia\" [2]\nKey advice:\n- Wikipedia is a **starting point, not an ending point** — useful for background, orientation, terminology, and finding bibliographies.\n- Researchers should **follow the footnotes/citations** in articles to reach the underlying primary and secondary sources, and verify claims independently.\n- Quality varies: users are told to check quality indicators (e.g., Good/Featured article status), review the **talk page**, edit history, and watch for vandalism, recent unsourced changes, and bias.\n- The page cautions that \"not everything in Wikipedia is accurate, comprehensive, or unbiased,\" and that completeness and currency differ across articles.\n\n### Related pages\n- **Wikipedia:Reliability** — states Wikipedia (or any wiki) should not be treated as a reliable source for its own content; reliability comes from the cited sources [3].\n- **Wikipedia:Academic use** — surveys scholarly debate on appropriate classroom/research use, consistently framing Wikipedia as a supplementary tool [4].\n- Wikipedia also maintains general and risk disclaimers advising independent verification of information [1].\n\n**Summary:** Wikipedia's own guidance is unambiguous — treat it as a tertiary source and research aid; do not cite it as an authority in academic work; instead use its references to trace and cite the primary and secondary sources.\n\nSources: [1] Wikipedia:Citing Wikipedia — https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Wikipedia:Citing_Wikipedia · [2] Wikipedia:Researching with Wikipedia — https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Wikipedia:Researching_with_Wikipedia · [3] Wikipedia:Reliability — https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Wikipedia:Reliability · [4] Wikipedia:Academic use — https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Wikipedia:Academic_use"